    <section id="menu">
        <h2>The menu</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Library</b>: everything in SilverScreen.db, with search, filters and rows of suggestions.</li>
            <li><b>Recently added</b>: what arrived on the NAS lately, newest first.</li>
            <li><b>Trending</b> and <b>Discover</b>: what's on TMDb, outside your library.</li>
            <li><b>Watchlists</b> and <b>Wishlist</b>: your lists. <b>Settings</b>: keys, Plex, folders and Export.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Most pages keep their choices in the address, so <b>Back</b> from a movie returns to the same list, and an address
            can be bookmarked or sent to a phone.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="library">
        <h2>Library</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Search library</b> finds titles in SilverScreen.db; an IMDb id works too. <b>Search OMDb</b> searches online,
                and <b>Import</b> on a hit puts it in the library (details only, no file).</li>
            <li><b>Filters</b>: genre, years, lowest IMDb rating, status (<b>Status - All</b>, Not watched or Watched; the last
                two ask Plex what you've seen, which takes a moment) and an actor or director.
                <b>Clear filters</b> takes them all away. The chips below choose movies or series.</li>
            <li>With no search, the page shows rows: <b>Recently added movies</b>, <b>Recently added series</b>,
                <b>Suggestions for you</b> and one row per genre, best rated first. <b>See all</b> on a row opens that genre;
                <b>Browse all</b> shows everything as one list with <b>Sort</b> and pages of 60.</li>
            <li><b>Suggestions for you</b> are movies like the ones you rated 3½ stars or more (see <a href="guide#ratings">My rating</a>).
                A movie like several of your favourites comes first; ones you have or have rated are left out. Movies like one you
                rated in the last week come first in the row, tagged <span class="tag fresh">New for you</span>, so a new
                rating shows at once (up to 8 of them); the rest of the 20 are picked from the best 40, a new pick each day. Hover a card to see which of your movies it's like. <b>See all</b> lists every suggestion, best first, with what each is like. The row is hidden for series,
                and until three of the movies you liked have been opened since the feature came (that's when their similar movies are kept).</li>
            <li>A card's stars at the bottom are your own rating; click them to go to it on the details page.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="details">
        <h2>A movie or series</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The top shows the IMDb rating (green from 7, orange from 6, red below), <b>In library</b>, <b>Watched</b> and the
                linked file. Genres and the names of actors and directors are links to the library filtered by them.</li>
            <li><b>▶ Play</b> plays the file in the browser, or in Plex (see <a href="guide#play">Play</a>).</li>
            <li><b>+ Add to watchlist</b> puts it on a Kino watchlist and/or the Plex playlist of that name.</li>
            <li><b>+ Add to wishlist</b> is there when you don't have the title with a file; then it says <b>On wishlist</b>.</li>
            <li><b>Update from OMDb</b> fetches the details again (<b>Import to library</b> for a title that isn't in it).
                <b>Mark as watched</b> marks it watched in Kino, and on Plex when Plex has it.</li>
            <li><b>★ My rating</b> opens your rating (see <a href="guide#ratings">My rating</a>).</li>
            <li><b>IMDb</b>, <b>TMDb</b> and <b>Plex</b> open the title there (Plex in Plex Web on app.plex.tv).</li>
            <li>The top takes its colours from the title's backdrop (else its poster), and shows the backdrop itself, faded, when
                KMDb has one.</li>
            <li><b>Similar movies</b> are TMDb's suggestions, with <b>+ Wishlist</b> to search for one on the wishlist page.
                They're kept for 30 days, so opening a movie again doesn't ask TMDb.</li>
            <li>A series lists its episodes by season, newest season first, each with <b>▶ Play</b> and <b>Plex</b>. A tag says
                whether it's complete; <b>Missing</b> episodes are listed in their place, with <b>+ Add to wishlist</b>
                (or <b>+ All … to wishlist</b>). They leave the wishlist by themselves when they arrive.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="play">
        <h2>Play</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Plays the movie's own file in the browser, with its subtitles (a .srt with the same name) when there are some.
                Not every format plays in a browser; <b>▶ Play in Plex</b> plays anything Plex has.</li>
            <li>For a series: <b>▶ First episode</b>, <b>▶ Latest episode</b>, and previous / next episode buttons.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="recent">
        <h2>Recently added</h2>
        <p>The library newest first, by when the files arrived on the NAS, under Today, Yesterday, Last 7 days and so on.
            The chips choose all, movies or series; the list can show only what you have or haven't watched.
            <b>Show more</b> adds 120 at a time.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="trending">
        <h2>Trending</h2>
        <p>What's trending on TMDb this week: <b>Movies</b>, <b>TV shows</b> or <b>People</b>, 20 of each. A title opens its
            details; a person opens the library filtered by them. Lists are kept a few hours; <b>Refresh</b> asks TMDb now.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="discover">
        <h2>Discover</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Movies or TV shows on TMDb for a season: <b>Winter</b>, <b>Spring and Easter</b>, <b>Summer</b>,
                <b>Autumn and Thanksgiving</b>, <b>Halloween</b> or <b>Christmas and New Year</b>. It opens on the season we're in.</li>
            <li>Narrow it with genre, years and rating (TMDb's users' average), and sort by <b>Most popular</b>,
                <b>Best rated</b> or <b>Newest</b>. <b>Hide what I have</b> leaves out what you have with a file.</li>
            <li>20 at a time; <b>Show more</b> fetches the next 20. Each page is kept a few hours; <b>Refresh</b> asks TMDb now.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="watchlists">
        <h2>Watchlists</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Pick a list at the top, or make one with <b>Create</b>. Filter by title, or show what you have or haven't watched.</li>
            <li>The watched button on a row marks it watched or not; <b>✕</b> removes it (and, if you say so, from the Plex playlist).</li>
            <li>A movie is on one watchlist at a time. Marked watched, it stays on its list; the <b>Watched</b> list shows every
                watched movie, from all lists.</li>
            <li><b>Export CSV</b> gives the list for Excel; <b>Copy to Plex playlist</b> puts it on the Plex playlist of the same name.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="wishlist">
        <h2>Wishlist</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Titles you want, perhaps without having them. <b>Search</b> looks in the library first, then OMDb and TMDb;
                write the year after a comma (<i>Kabul, 2025</i>) to keep to it, or use an IMDb id. <b>+ Wishlist</b> adds a hit.</li>
            <li>The list shows those to get first. A title counts as yours only with a file; one in the library without a file is still to get.</li>
            <li><b>Download CSV</b> gives the list for Excel, with In SilverScreen as true/false; the PC's Wishlist can import it.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="ratings">
        <h2>My rating</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>On a details page: five stars in halves, as on Plex; the left half of a star is half a star. Add a review if you like,
                then <b>Save</b>. <b>Remove rating</b> takes it away.</li>
            <li>The rating is set on Plex too, where only you see it. The review stays in Kino.</li>
            <li>Rated titles show your stars on the library's cards. Movies you rate 3½ stars or more feed <b>Suggestions for you</b>.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="settings">
        <h2>Settings and Export</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Movie services</b>: your OMDb and TMDb keys (the shared default keys work, but share OMDb's daily limit with
                everyone). <b>Plex</b>: server name and token. <b>Test connections</b> tries them before you <b>Save</b>.</li>
            <li><b>Uranus and KMDb</b>: Uranus URL and token, and the <b>KMDb URL</b>. With KMDb the library is in MariaDB, shared
                with Kino on the PC; the box <b>Library</b> shows whether MariaDB (KMDb) or the LiteDB file is in use, and
                <b>Check KMDb now</b> looks again. While KMDb doesn't answer the LiteDB file is used, and the changes are sent to
                KMDb when it is back. MariaDB is the better choice when it is available.</li>
            <li><b>Folders</b> are shared with SilverScreen and Kino on the PC, so they're usually Windows paths.</li>
            <li><b>Export</b> (needed only without KMDb): <b>Export ratings</b> and <b>Import ratings…</b> carry your ratings between the NAS and the PC
                (in Kino on the PC: Settings → Importer vurderinger fra fil); the newer rating of a title wins.
                <b>Export wishlist</b> gives the wishlist as CSV.</li>
            <li>When it's set up, the wishlist and your ratings are also sent to problembar.net by themselves a few seconds after each change.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="colours">
        <h2>Colours and tags</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><span class="tag good">In SilverScreen</span> in the library, with a file that's there.</li>
            <li><span class="tag nofile">In SilverScreen, no file</span> in the library without one, e.g. after <b>Import to library</b>.</li>
            <li>IMDb ratings: <span class="rating high">7.0+</span>, <span class="rating medium">6.0–6.9</span>, <span class="rating low">below 6</span>.</li>
            <li>A green <b>Watched</b> tag: played to the end on Plex, or marked watched on a watchlist.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="good-to-know">
        <h2>Good to know</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>The NAS has its own copy of the library. When a new copy is sent from the PC, it replaces the NAS's, watched marks
                and ratings set here included; export your ratings first and import them on the PC.</li>
            <li>The app has no login: use it on your home network.</li>
            <li>TMDb and OMDb answers are kept for a while (Trending and Discover a few hours, similar movies 30 days), to save
                calls; <b>Refresh</b> where there is one asks again.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><a href="settings">‹ Back to Settings</a></p>
    </section>
</article>
